x761 puts x759 x760
x762 call open x761 4
x763 write x762 2
x764 text "use \"ui.a0\"\nuse \"../examples/life.a0\"\nfn page io -> io\na call open p0 5\nb call close a\nret b\nend"
x765 puts x763 x764
x766 call close x765 
x767 call close x766 
x768 call close x767 
x769 call open x768 13
x770 write x769 4
x771 write x770 1
x772 text "editing"
x773 puts x771 x772
x774 call open x773 11
x775 write x774 2
x776 text "Views and edits"
x777 puts x775 x776
x778 call close x777 
x779 call open x778 2
x780 write x779 2
x781 text "A model never sees a whole file. It asks for a view of one function: the function with a handle line on top (e0), one signature line per callee, and a program handle (g0). The program view is scoped: a comment line with the function count, then the signatures of the target, its transitive callees, and its direct callers, so it stays the same size as the program grows to thousands of functions. A reply is the handle line followed by edits, one per line: `id op ...` replaces the instruction id or inserts it before ret; `id op ... @ other` inserts after other; `-id` deletes; `ret x` changes the result. Under g0, a whole `fn ... end` block adds or replaces a function and `-fn name` removes one. The edit is applied only if it parses, type-checks, validates, and was written against the current revision; otherwise it is rejected and the handle stays valid."
x782 puts x780 x781
x783 call close x782 
x784 call open x783 10
x785 write x784 4
x786 write x785 2
x787 text "code"
x788 puts x786 x787
x789 call open x788 4
x790 call open x789 6
x791 write x790 4
x792 write x791 2
x793 text "cm"
x794 puts x792 x793
x795 write x794 2
x796 text "# a view of clamp, then a reply that fixes the upper bound\n"
x797 puts x795 x796
x798 call close x797 
x799 write x798 2
x800 text "e0 fn clamp u32 u32 u32 -> u32\nlo lt p0 p1\nhi lt p2 p0\na select lo p1 p0\nr select hi p2 a\nret r\n\n"
x801 puts x799 x800
x802 call open x801 6
x803 write x802 4
x804 write x803 2
x805 text "cm"
x806 puts x804 x805
x807 write x806 2
x808 text "# reply\n"
x809 puts x807 x808
x810 call close x809 
x811 write x810 2
x812 text "e0\nhi lt p2 a\nr select hi p2 a"
x813 puts x811 x812
x814 call close x813 
x815 call close x814 
x816 call close x815 
x817 call open x816 13
x818 write x817 4
x819 write x818 1
x820 text "diagnostics"
x821 puts x819 x820
x822 call open x821 11
x823 write x822 2
x824 text "Diagnostics"
x825 puts x823 x824
x826 call close x825 
x827 call open x826 2
x828 write x827 2
x829 text "Every rejection carries a stable code (parse, type, structure, handle, revision, limit), what was expected, what was seen, and the one fix that resolves it. The compiler never guesses: a reply that is ambiguous is rejected with the fix, not applied approximately."
x830 puts x828 x829
x831 call close x830 
x832 call close x831 
ret x832
end

fn sec_run io -> io
x834 call open p0 13
x835 write x834 4
x836 write x835 1
x837 text "bounds"
x838 puts x836 x837
x839 call open x838 11
x840 write x839 2
x841 text "Bounds and safety"
x842 puts x840 x841
x843 call close x842 
x844 call open x843 2
x845 write x844 2
x846 text "Static caps: 65536 functions per program (also after linking), 4096 instructions per function, 64 parameters, arrays up to 65536 elements (1024 on the hardware and GPU targets), literal iteration budget 2^24 per function. Variable iteration counts are bounded by fuel in the reference interpreter; compiled targets have no execution budget. io output is bounded by the caller's buffer. There is no heap, no recursion, no exceptions, and no undefined behavior: div and rem by zero are defined, shifts mask the count, indices wrap modulo the array length."
x847 puts x845 x846
x848 call close x847 
x849 call close x848 
x850 call open x849 13
x851 write x850 4
x852 write x851 1
x853 text "cli"
